Where the recipe lives in a config Config key Registry What it decides When it runs <code>optimizer</code> <code>OPTIMIZERS</code> how gradients become weight updates once per <code>train.accumulate</code> batches <code>scheduler</code> <code>SCHEDULERS</code> the learning rate over the run after every optimizer step or every epoch (<code>train.scheduler_unit</code>) <code>hooks</code> <code>HOOKS</code> anything else around the loop: weight averages, freezing, clipping variants at <code>before_run</code>, <code>before_epoch</code>, <code>before_step</code>, <code>after_iter</code>, <code>after_epoch</code>, <code>after_run</code> <code>data.train.dataset.transforms</code> <code>TRANSFORMS</code> augmentation of one training sample in the data-loader workers, per sample <p>Plain options of the loop need no component: <code>train.amp</code>, <code>train.accumulate</code>, <code>train.grad_clip</code> with <code>train.grad_clip_mode</code>, <code>train.ema</code>, <code>train.sync_bn</code>. Three method-neutral files under <code>configs/_base_/schedules/</code> can be inherited without compose: <code>adamw_onecycle_10e.yaml</code>, <code>adamw_warmup-cosine_10e.yaml</code>, <code>schedulefree-adamw_10e.yaml</code>.</p> Input and output <ul> <li>Optimizer. Built from the <code>optimizer</code> node; the engine supplies <code>params</code> (or what the   detector's <code>configure_optimizers</code> returns) and, where asked, <code>model</code>. The bridges return   the library's own optimizer, so checkpoints hold its state unchanged.</li> <li>Scheduler. Built per optimizer; the trainer steps it per optimizer step   (<code>train.scheduler_unit: iteration</code>) or per epoch. The engine fills in the length of the   run where a schedule needs it. <code>scheduler: null</code> is a constant rate.</li> <li>Hook. A subclass of <code>grasppanda.engine.hooks.Hook</code>; every callback receives the   training state. Hooks run in ascending <code>priority</code> (evaluation is 70, checkpointing 80). A   weight average installs itself as <code>state.ema</code>, which <code>evaluation.weights: ema</code> scores.</li> <li>Transform. Maps one <code>PointSample</code> to one <code>PointSample</code>. It may remove or reorder points   only through <code>sample.gather(index)</code>, which carries the labels; move the scene only with the   object poses; and not move points at all when the sample carries images, as HGGD's does   (the config check refuses that list). Scaling the scene is not allowed: grasp widths,   depths and the gripper are in metres.</li> <li>The config check tests every key against the constructor of the named class without   importing the optional libraries; free-form arguments of a library go into <code>options</code>. A   wrong optimizer name is found when the optimizer is built, with the list of valid ones.</li> </ul> Optimizers and parameter groups <pre><code>optimizer: {type: AdamW, lr: 1.0e-3, weight_decay: 0.05}                    # torch.optim, by class
optimizer: {type: TimmOptimizer, name: lamb, lr: 2.0e-3, weight_decay: 0.02}
optimizer: {type: PytorchOptimizer, name: prodigy, lr: 1.0}
optimizer: {type: ScheduleFree, name: adamw, lr: 2.5e-3, warmup_steps: 200}
</code></pre> <ul> <li><code>lr</code> and <code>weight_decay</code> are arguments of the bridge; everything else (<code>betas</code>, <code>eps</code>,   <code>momentum</code>) goes into <code>options</code>. A <code>lookahead_</code> prefix wraps any timm optimizer.</li> <li>A schedule-free optimizer needs <code>scheduler: null</code> and <code>ScheduleFreeHook</code>; its presets set   all three.</li> </ul> <p><code>ParamGroups</code> wraps any optimizer with a rate per module and layer-wise decay:</p> <pre><code>optimizer:
  type: ParamGroups
  optimizer: {type: AdamW, lr: 1.0e-3, weight_decay: 0.05}
  lr_multipliers: {backbone: 0.1}      # dotted prefix of the parameter name, or "re:&lt;regex&gt;"
  layer_decay: 0.75                    # null: off
  layer_decay_module: backbone
  layer_decay_depth: 2
  no_decay_1d: true                    # no weight decay on biases and normalisation weights
</code></pre> <p>The rate of a parameter is <code>lr</code> × the first matching multiplier × <code>layer_decay ** (L - i)</code> for layer <code>i</code> of the <code>L</code> layers of <code>layer_decay_module</code>, counted in registration order (a U-Net's decoder counts as later layers). To train a pretrained encoder, load its weights with <code>runtime.load_from=&lt;checkpoint&gt;</code> and <code>runtime.allow_weight_mismatch=true</code>, then use the encoder presets.</p> Schedules, hooks and augmentation <pre><code>scheduler: {type: OneCycleLR, max_lr: 1.0e-3, pct_start: 0.05, div_factor: 10.0, cycle_momentum: false}
scheduler: {type: TimmSchedule, name: cosine, warmup_steps: 0.05, warmup_lr_init: 1.0e-6, lr_min: 1.0e-6}
hooks:
  - {type: EMAHook, decay: 0.999, warmup: true, interval: 1, buffers: copy}
  - {type: FreezeHook, modules: [backbone], epochs: 2, eval_mode: true}
evaluation: {weights: ema}
</code></pre> <ul> <li><code>TimmSchedule.warmup_steps</code> below 1 is a fraction of the run, from 1 a step count;   <code>warmup_lr_init</code> and <code>lr_min</code> are absolute rates.</li> <li><code>EMAHook</code>, <code>SWAHook</code> and <code>train.ema</code> exclude each other. <code>EMAHook</code> warms its decay up and   copies buffers by default (batch-norm statistics and HGGD's learned anchors stay those of   the trained model).</li> <li><code>FreezeHook</code> sets <code>requires_grad</code> per epoch, so nothing is rebuilt when modules are   released. <code>AdaptiveGradClipHook</code> and <code>train.grad_clip</code> exclude each other.</li> <li><code>--set augment=dropout+shift+jitter</code> writes <code>RandomDropout</code> before the sampling step and   <code>RandomShift</code> and <code>RandomJitter</code> after the base list. Units are metres in the camera frame;   <code>RandomJitter</code> perturbs only the network input.</li> </ul> Components and their sources <p><code>pip install -e ".[optim]"</code> installs timm, pytorch_optimizer and schedulefree (all Apache-2.0); the rest needs nothing beyond the core install.</p> Component Kind What it is Code <code>TimmOptimizer</code>, <code>TimmSchedule</code> optimizer, schedule any optimizer or schedule of timm by <code>name</code> bridge to <code>timm.optim</code>, <code>timm.scheduler</code> <code>PytorchOptimizer</code> optimizer any optimizer of pytorch_optimizer by <code>name</code> bridge to <code>pytorch_optimizer.load_optimizer</code> <code>ScheduleFree</code>, <code>ScheduleFreeHook</code> optimizer, hook schedule-free AdamW, RAdam or SGD (arXiv 2405.15682) and the switch between its training point and its average bridge to <code>schedulefree</code> <code>ParamGroups</code> optimizer rate per module, layer-wise decay (BEiT, arXiv 2106.08254), no decay on 1-D parameters written here classes of <code>torch.optim</code> and <code>torch.optim.lr_scheduler</code> optimizer, schedule <code>AdamW</code>, <code>SGD</code>, <code>OneCycleLR</code>, <code>CosineAnnealingLR</code>, … PyTorch <code>WarmupCosineLR</code> schedule linear warm-up then cosine, in closed form (FineGrasp's schedule) written here <code>EMAHook</code>, <code>SWAHook</code> hook weight averages (arXiv 1703.01780, arXiv 1803.05407) <code>torch.optim.swa_utils.AveragedModel</code> <code>FreezeHook</code> hook freeze sub-modules for the first epochs (gradual unfreezing, arXiv 1801.06146) written here <code>AdaptiveGradClipHook</code> hook adaptive gradient clipping (arXiv 2102.06171) bridge to <code>timm.utils.adaptive_clip_grad</code> <code>RandomJitter</code>, <code>RandomDropout</code>, <code>RandomShift</code> transform noise on the points, removal of points with their labels, a shift of the scene and the object poses adapted from Pointcept @ 1342eda (MIT) Pitfalls met while building this <ul> <li><code>OneCycleLR</code> and momentum. Its default <code>cycle_momentum: true</code> rewrites <code>betas</code> or   <code>momentum</code> every step; the presets set <code>false</code>. Its peak is <code>max_lr</code>: changing   <code>optimizer.lr</code> changes nothing.</li> <li>Schedule-free and batch norm. The evaluated weights are an average, but the batch-norm   statistics were collected at the training point; this toolbox does not re-estimate them.</li> <li>Short probes and warm-ups. With <code>warmup_steps: 200</code> a 20-iteration probe runs inside   the warm-up, so its losses fall slowly; that says nothing about a full run.</li> <li>Per-epoch units and warm-up. With <code>train.scheduler_unit: epoch</code> a warm-up from 0   spends the first epoch at rate 0; <code>multistep-warmup</code> starts at 1e-4.</li> <li>Lists replace. <code>hooks</code> and <code>transforms</code> of a child config replace the inherited list;   compose writes the base's entries out again, a hand-written child must repeat them (HGGD   needs its <code>EpochSignalHook</code>).</li> <li><code>SWAHook.start_epoch</code> must be below <code>train.epochs</code>; the run refuses to start otherwise.   A short probe ends before averaging starts, so such a probe says nothing about fit.</li> <li>Mixed precision is refused for the ported methods. When it was tried, the label   assigner of the seed-based skeleton wrote half-precision values into a single-precision   tensor, and HGGD's first loss was not finite. <code>hooks=amp</code> sets <code>train.amp: true</code>, which the   config check now refuses unless the detector declares <code>amp_ready = True</code>; none of the   shipped detectors does. The preset is the switch for a detector of your own.</li> <li>A wrong optimizer name passes the config check and fails when the optimizer is built,   with the list of valid names.</li> </ul> Not wrapped Candidate Why not Normalisation and activation as config keys no shared layer builder exists in the stages; a hook that swapped modules after construction would not run at test time. <code>RandomScale</code>, <code>ElasticDistortion</code> grasp widths, depths and the gripper are in metres, and labels attach to rigid object poses. Chromatic transforms the scene encoders read no colour; HGGD's image pipeline is not a point transform. Mix3D scene mixing works in the collate function, and two scenes' labels would have to be merged. Sharpness-aware minimisation (SAM) its <code>step()</code> needs a second forward and backward pass. <code>ReduceLROnPlateau</code> steps on a monitored metric, which the trainer does not hand to schedules; refused by name. Batch-norm re-estimation after SWA <code>update_bn</code> iterates plain tensors; batches here are <code>Batch</code> objects.

GraspNet-baselineGSNetScale-Balanced-GraspEconomicGraspFineGraspHGGD <pre><code>grasppanda test \
    graspnet_baseline/graspnet-baseline_pointnet2_g1b-realsense-to-gc6d-d435_18e \
    --weights &lt;weights of graspnet-baseline_pointnet2_g1b-realsense_18e&gt;
</code></pre> <pre><code>grasppanda test \
    gsnet/gsnet_sunet14_g1b-realsense-to-gc6d-d435_10e \
    --weights &lt;weights of gsnet_sunet14_g1b-realsense_10e&gt;
</code></pre> <pre><code>grasppanda test \
    scale_balanced_grasp/sbg_pointnet2-transformer_g1b-realsense-to-gc6d-d435_18e \
    --weights &lt;weights of sbg_pointnet2-transformer_g1b-realsense_18e&gt;
</code></pre> <pre><code>grasppanda test \
    economicgrasp/economicgrasp_sunet14_g1b-realsense-to-gc6d-d435_10e \
    --weights &lt;weights of economicgrasp_sunet14_g1b-realsense_10e&gt;
grasppanda test \
    economicgrasp/economicgrasp_sunet14_g1b-kinect-to-gc6d-d435_10e \
    --weights &lt;weights of economicgrasp_sunet14_g1b-kinect_10e&gt;
</code></pre> <p>No transfer config ships for FineGrasp yet, and FineGrasp has not been tested on GraspClutter6D here. A transfer config is a short child of the method config; the fold Why a config and not a list of overrides below shows one.</p> <pre><code>grasppanda test \
    hggd/hggd_r34w8_g1b-realsense-to-gc6d-d435_15e \
    --weights &lt;weights of hggd_r34w8_g1b-realsense_15e&gt;
</code></pre> <p>HGGD reads the whole image, as in training: no ground truth shapes its input.</p> <p>Each transfer config scores the RealSense D435 frames of the <code>test</code> split: 235 scenes × 13 views = 3 055 frames. A run took 8 to 12 minutes per method on the development machine (one GPU, shared with other jobs). The result records say <code>protocol: graspclutter6d-sv-v1</code>, the camera, the variant and <code>gt_assisted</code>.</p>
